%N A398684 Decimal expansion of two-dimensional minimum-variance onion constant.
%C A398684 The two-dimensional (infinity, infinity) onion problem considers the continuous limit of an onion of radius 1 modeled as infinitely many concentric semicircular layers r in (0, 1] sitting on a flat cutting board (y = 0). Infinitely many radial knife cuts are made through the onion, aiming toward a single target point located at depth h directly below the origin (0, -h), dividing the onion into infinitesimal pieces.
%C A398684 The two-dimensional minimum-variance onion constant, denoted by samekh_2^var, is the target depth h (measured in onion radii) that minimizes the variance of the relative areas of these infinitesimal onion pieces.
%C A398684 The discrete problem was posed by Chef J. Kenji López-Alt in 2020. This exact numerical value (0.557306...) was featured in his June 12, 2024 New York Times column "What Is the Best Way to Cut an Onion?". Minimizing the coefficient of variation (standard deviation divided by mean) instead yields samekh_2^cv = 0.757425...
%H A398684 Dylan Poulsen, <a href="/A398684/b398684.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..9999</a>
%H A398684 Andrew Aquino, Russell Samora, and Jan Diehm, <a href="https://pudding.cool/2025/08/onions/">Dicing an Onion, the Mathematically Optimal Way</a>, The Pudding, August 2025.
%H A398684 J. Kenji López-Alt, <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/article/how-to-cut-onion.html">What Is the Best Way to Cut an Onion?</a>, The New York Times, June 12, 2024.
%H A398684 Dylan Poulsen, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1080/0025570X.2025.2521248">How to Cut An Onion Optimally: A Love Letter to the Jacobian</a>, Mathematics Magazine (2025).
%F A398684 Equals the unique root in (0, 1) of f(h) = (-3*Pi^2*log(1 + 1/h^2) + 4*log(1 + 1/h^2)*L(h)*(h + 2*Pi + 2*h^3*log(2*h) - 2*(1-h^2)^(3/2)*arccos(h)) + 24*L(h)^2*(1 + 2*h^2*log(2*h) + 2*h*sqrt(1-h^2)*arccos(h))) / (48*L(h)^3) where L(h) = arccot(h) - (h/2)*log(1 + 1/h^2).
%e A398684 0.557306692985664478851093...
%t A398684 L[h_] := ArcCot[h] - 1/2 h Log[1 + 1/h^2];
%t A398684 f[h_] := (-3 Pi^2 Log[1 + 1/h^2] +
%t A398684      4 Log[1 + 1/h^2] L[
%t A398684        h] (h + 2 Pi + 2 h^3 Log[2 h] - 2 (1 - h^2)^(3/2) ArcCos[h]) +
%t A398684      24 L[h]^2 (1 + 2 h^2 Log[2 h] +
%t A398684         2 h Sqrt[1 - h^2] ArcCos[h]))/(48 L[h]^3);
%t A398684 RealDigits[h /. FindRoot[f[h] == 0, {h, 1/2}, WorkingPrecision -> 230],
%t A398684    10, 99][[1]]
%K A398684 nonn,cons,new
%O A398684 0,1
%A A398684 _Dylan Poulsen_, Aug 06 2026
